    "product_id": "640994bf-2c17-4d98-9b7d-8f5ef123501c",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 231221\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n420 AM PST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Breezy winds are expected midweek with isolated wind impacts \n  possible in the Colorado River Valley.\n\n* Warming trend expected through the week as below normal \n  temperature today climb to above normal by Friday. Temperatures \n  will remain above normal despite the potential for another weak \n  system this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nQuiet weather is expected much of the work week. A shortwave moving \nthrough today will mainly bring clouds, no impacts are expected. \nRidging with occasional shortwaves moving through the flow are \nexpected through the week which will result in dry conditions and \nwarming temperatures. A piece of energy moves through around midweek \nwhich will bring breezy winds Wednesday and Thursday afternoons. \nProbabilities for impactful wind gusts over 40 MPH remain under 10% \nacross much of the region outside the Colorado River Valley where \nnorth winds will be enhanced down the valley. NBM does show 70%-80% \nprobabilities for wind gusts over 40 MPH in the lower Colorado River \nValley, but it may just be isolated around the Laughlin/Bullhead \nCity area. Boating may be impacted with winds and waves. By Friday, \nwinds will diminish as the shortwave quickly exits the region.\n\nWarming temperatures are expected through the week as ridging builds \nin between each system, temperatures will warm warm through the \nweek. By the end of the work week, temperatures should climb a few \ndegrees above normal. Despite another quick system moving through \nthis weekend that could produce another uptick in winds and low \nchances for precipitation, it should remain above normal through the \nweekend. Low confidence with the late weekend system and if it will \nproduce wind and/or rain and snow impacts, however at this time no \nsignificant impacts are expected as the system would be weak. The \nunsettled weather pattern continues into early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...\nWinds will remain light and somewhat variable through the forecast \nperiod, but will generally follow daily directional patterns.\nWinds favor a NE direction this afternoon, then  back to a SW \ndirection tonight with speeds at or below 8 kt. Satellite imagery \nshows clearing skies across the Valley with high clouds at or above \n25 kft approaching from the west tonight. VFR conditions are \nexpected to continue.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds across the region \nwill remain under 10KT and generally follow typical daily \ndirectional patterns with some variability. KDAG is an exception \nwhere W winds 9-12 kt continue through this afternoon before \ndiminishing. At KIFP, terrain enhancement may result in northerly \nwinds 8-12 kt this afternoon and tonight with isolated higher gusts \npossible. Otherwise, generally quiet and calm at most terminals with \nmid and high level clouds remaining at VFR levels. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
